{% extends '@theme/layout.twig' %}
{% set page_title = 'Research — Sara Bonella' %}
{% set meta_description = 'Sara Bonella develops computational methods for molecular simulation, quantum dynamics, non-equilibrium physics, and electrochemical interfaces.' %}
{% set og_title = 'Research — Matter in motion' %}
{% set og_description = meta_description %}
{% set body_class = 'page-research' %}

{% block content %}
    {% set hero_kind = 'research' %}
    {% set hero_canvas = true %}
    {% set hero_eyebrow = 'Research' %}
    {% set hero_title = 'Making the invisible calculable' %}
    {% set hero_deck = 'Sara develops controlled approximations and simulation methods for physical behaviour that is too small, fast, rare, or complex to observe directly.' %}
    {% set hero_roles = ['Molecular and statistical physics', 'Approximate quantum dynamics', 'Electrochemical interfaces and scientific computing'] %}
    {% set hero_primary_url = '#questions' %}
    {% set hero_primary_label = 'Explore the questions' %}
    {% set hero_secondary_url = '/publications' %}
    {% set hero_secondary_label = 'Selected publications' %}
    {% set hero_caption = 'Underdamped Langevin particles crossing a double-well free-energy landscape' %}
    {% set hero_source_url = 'https://doi.org/10.1063/5.0169050' %}
    {% include '@theme/orbital-hero.twig' %}

    <section class="section" aria-labelledby="approach-title">
        <div class="bio-grid shell">
            <div class="bio-lead">
                <p class="eyebrow">Her approach</p>
                <h2 id="approach-title">Useful answers begin with honest approximations</h2>
            </div>
            <div class="bio-copy">
                <p>Much of computational physics begins with an impossible request: follow every relevant degree of freedom, at every scale, without losing the structure of the underlying physics. Sara’s work asks where that description can be simplified—and what must be preserved for the result to remain meaningful.</p>
                <p>This has led her from mixed quantum-classical dynamics and nuclear quantum effects to non-equilibrium statistical mechanics, rare events, electrochemical interfaces, and constrained molecular dynamics. The systems change; the discipline of connecting method, observable, and physical interpretation does not.</p>
                <p class="lede" style="margin-top:2rem;">Approximation is not a shortcut around physics; it is a precise statement about which parts of the problem carry the answer.</p>
            </div>
        </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section section--field" id="questions" aria-labelledby="questions-title">
        <div class="research-feature__grid shell">
            <div>
                <p class="eyebrow">Questions in motion</p>
                <h2 id="questions-title">Three recurring lines of enquiry</h2>
                <div class="question-list">
                    <article class="question-row" id="quantum">
                        <span>01</span>
                        <div><h3>How much quantum behaviour can a practical simulation retain?</h3><p>Trajectory-based approximations, quantum thermal baths, and correlation functions connect formal quantum descriptions with realistic condensed-phase systems.</p></div>
                    </article>
                    <article class="question-row" id="constraints">
                        <span>02</span>
                        <div><h3>Can a constraint become a computational advantage?</h3><p>Mass-Zero constrained dynamics—MaZe—handles fast electronic or polarization variables without hiding the physical assumptions that make the method work.</p></div>
                    </article>
                    <article class="question-row" id="interfaces">
                        <span>03</span>
                        <div><h3>What happens at a driven molecular interface?</h3><p>Statistical mechanics and molecular dynamics reveal organisation, transport, rare events, and phase behaviour at charged interfaces and away from equilibrium.</p></div>
                    </article>
                </div>
            </div>
            <div class="research-study">
                <p class="eyebrow">A common thread</p>
                <h3>Preserve the physics that carries the answer.</h3>
                <p class="section-deck" style="margin-top:1rem;">A faster calculation is useful only when its approximations are visible, testable, and appropriate to the question being asked.</p>
                <div class="action-row"><a class="text-action" href="mailto:sara.bonella@epfl.ch?subject=Research%20collaboration">Discuss a research question <span aria-hidden="true">→</span></a></div>
            </div>
        </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section" aria-labelledby="methods-title">
        <div class="teaching-grid shell">
            <div>
                <p class="eyebrow">Methods &amp; systems</p>
                <h2 id="methods-title">From formal ideas to working simulations</h2>
                <p class="section-deck" style="margin-top:1.4rem;">Method development and physical application move together: each exposes what the other still needs to explain.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="course-list">
                <article class="course-item"><small>Dynamics</small><h3>Quantum and mixed quantum-classical methods</h3><p>Practical descriptions of time-dependent behaviour and nuclear quantum effects.</p></article>
                <article class="course-item"><small>Statistics</small><h3>Non-equilibrium systems and rare events</h3><p>Fluctuation relations, free-energy reconstruction, transport, and driven matter.</p></article>
                <article class="course-item"><small>Simulation</small><h3>MaZe and constrained molecular dynamics</h3><p>Algorithms for electronic, polarization, and multiscale variables with separated time scales.</p></article>
                <article class="course-item"><small>Interfaces</small><h3>Condensed phases and electrochemistry</h3><p>Molecular organisation and phase behaviour where electrolytes meet metallic electrodes.</p></article>
            </div>
        </div>
    </section>

{% if entries|length > 0 %}
    <section class="section section--field" aria-labelledby="research-notes-title">
        <div class="shell">
            <div class="section-heading section-heading--split">
                <div><p class="eyebrow">Current programme</p><h2 id="research-notes-title">Research notes</h2></div>
                <p class="section-deck">Focused introductions to active areas of work.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="area-orbit">
                {% for item in entries %}
                    <article class="research-card">
                        <p class="eyebrow">{{ item.field('category')|default('Computational physics', true) }}</p>
                        <h3>{{ item.field('title') }}</h3>
                        <p>{{ item.field('summary') }}</p>
                        <a class="card-link" href="/research/{{ item.slug }}">Read the research note →</a>
                    </article>
                {% endfor %}
            </div>
        </div>
    </section>
{% endif %}

    <section class="section section--field" aria-labelledby="starting-points-title">
        <div class="shell">
            <div class="section-heading section-heading--split">
                <div><p class="eyebrow">Starting points</p><h2 id="starting-points-title">Three recent papers</h2></div>
                <p class="section-deck">A phase transition at an interface, a new constrained-dynamics formulation, and its high-performance implementation.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="selected-work">
                <article class="selected-paper"><time>2025</time><div><h3>Electrically driven first-order phase transition of a 2D ionic crystal at the electrode/electrolyte interface</h3><p>Electrochemical interfaces · PNAS</p></div><a href="https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.2520026122" target="_blank" rel="noopener">DOI ↗</a></article>
                <article class="selected-paper"><time>2025</time><div><h3>mass-zero constrained molecular dynamics for electrostatic interactions</h3><p>Method development · The Journal of Chemical Physics</p></div><a href="https://doi.org/10.1063/5.0283356" target="_blank" rel="noopener">DOI ↗</a></article>
                <article class="selected-paper"><time>2025</time><div><h3>A tale of two codes: CUDA vs OpenACC for mass-zero constrained dynamics</h3><p>Scientific software · International Journal of High Performance Computing Applications</p></div><a href="https://doi.org/10.1177/10943420251331673" target="_blank" rel="noopener">DOI ↗</a></article>
            </div>
            <div class="action-row"><a class="button" href="/publications">Browse selected publications</a></div>
        </div>
    </section>

    <section class="contact-invitation" aria-labelledby="research-contact-title">
        <div class="contact-invitation__grid shell">
            <div><p class="eyebrow">Collaboration</p><h2 id="research-contact-title">Bring the physical question first.</h2><p>For a possible collaboration, seminar, or method discussion, share the system, the observable, and the computational challenge.</p></div>
            <a class="button" href="mailto:sara.bonella@epfl.ch?subject=Research%20collaboration">Write to Sara</a>
        </div>
    </section>
{% endblock %}
